One season, the city faced a drought of kindness. People hustled through streets, headphones on, eyes down, missing the laughter that should rise between stalls. Lio opened a passage between his pots, a winding stair of greens that led toward the heart of the market. He invited everyone to plant a wish in a new pot and water it with a single kind act—a compliment to a stranger, a shared umbrella, a five-minute chat with a neighbor.

As days passed, the pots sprouted not only herbs but conversations. A baker learned a shopper’s name; a courier stopped to listen to an elderly painter. The market flowered with small rituals of care: a pot of mint for neighbors, a tomato for a worried teacher, herbs for a child who felt unseen. The rain finally fell, but this time it blessed a city that had learned to water itself with attention.

